#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb96cd29-b9f6-5b68-b43e-4a55ef8ffab2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Linear Map"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8122d5-f12e-5928-bff2-d5fa1e48f157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388689} {:answer/id #uuid "f3406d83-bcf3-596a-a8ff-175ed945db82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388690} {:answer/id #uuid "a133313d-ecad-58e1-843b-34dbf5fd3caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388691} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a1be2d-8799-5b95-95c0-dd8a87b62661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}29 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388692} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9fadbf-8e26-50d8-9e73-eb05439ff5e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b9fadbf-8e26-50d8-9e73-eb05439ff5e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388693} :answer-field/id #uuid "237ffe5e-29b0-5d76-bc76-f812ffd3794e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "913716b3-e8d7-57a6-a016-07aa651d8ad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140424" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ find $T(v).$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $\\,T(v)=T\\cdot v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0587fad6-5ef2-5200-9b60-ddbd0bc21ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases Given One Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088d91a0-3eaf-51f2-8ffe-b7c678ccdbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388696} {:answer/id #uuid "a023134e-086b-501d-b434-5e8d31a4adbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388697} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0e6d22-1976-5811-872d-d23e457baf1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388698} {:answer/id #uuid "ad10460a-fb61-5b63-8b5e-2777f724a5aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388699} {:answer/id #uuid "37fb40f0-005c-5d6e-98d1-6dd223500ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0e6d22-1976-5811-872d-d23e457baf1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388698} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d8dbb4a-8123-5815-85e7-0b6642b71201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c5b8550-93bd-5bca-bdbb-d5d6c011cb4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140441" :question/problem "Consider the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84947238-f446-5261-8ecd-ee263f64e04f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Linear Map Given the Matrix Relative to Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d47586c1-ff78-5ba9-89e3-901a684b6222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388703} {:answer/id #uuid "07fa398f-3d09-56e7-85cb-123bab13520b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388704} {:answer/id #uuid "a68f996f-a99f-5e16-9602-c7c8e8063308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388705} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf6f432-a587-5488-bc34-c2eed0919014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ 24 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388706} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1fdfdb-bfc3-553f-a1fe-9885d23bb96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -27 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d47586c1-ff78-5ba9-89e3-901a684b6222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388703} :answer-field/id #uuid "c05b417e-8d43-599f-9a29-d3bb1aa97f32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59fd0156-bcfb-5880-8b9a-95479297119c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140445" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39af9a4f-5bb0-5f67-b99e-b356fdeef13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388710} {:answer/id #uuid "24019f33-d58e-5c2e-9bc0-2466a9447e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & 36 \\\\ -25 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388711} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb9cc07-a4ed-5da6-b193-1c9894ee42b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -25 \\\\ 20 & -36\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388712} {:answer/id #uuid "1667976f-4e72-5f00-aab0-1b8c12345f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -36 \\\\ 20 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388713} {:answer/id #uuid "d77416b1-f86b-5a81-a593-653abb91c3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39af9a4f-5bb0-5f67-b99e-b356fdeef13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388710} :answer-field/id #uuid "6811151d-cd95-5e12-aa34-9045fdbee48f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fe32d4d-9284-57a0-89e5-58bdfd98337d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140454" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d37da179-9615-5450-a7b3-93b7aa051302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388717} {:answer/id #uuid "f317900b-8e9b-5d9e-9c0d-6ff0446e9fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388718} {:answer/id #uuid "2342b5cf-f0c7-5a89-82ee-5efc962266ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388719} {:answer/id #uuid "21002614-dd8a-5c31-b133-02b93a07c01a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388720} {:answer/id #uuid "3a361f9f-9e4d-5902-b1b8-055b8e6d3c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2342b5cf-f0c7-5a89-82ee-5efc962266ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388719} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a64c1b-68af-5bf0-a02a-43ac61c6cd84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de22f8a7-0462-58b6-8ab0-2cf50f198a6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140466" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=15,$ and $c=6.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{15+6}{1}=21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de10cf8a-735b-57e8-ae48-8c03659228a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388724} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6df5a9-646c-5f06-b423-afa48dc499cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388725} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d039de-fb02-5736-a8a0-936338029fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388726} {:answer/id #uuid "3739a38d-94f8-558b-babe-12de92f70ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388727} {:answer/id #uuid "2053cc0b-51be-50d9-861e-6da8abfee243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d039de-fb02-5736-a8a0-936338029fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388726} :answer-field/id #uuid "56149026-6abc-5c7c-a826-b1d92a72a9c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d9b6de3-4396-5c82-b41c-2429c7a1964b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140472" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c34ca5fe-08f4-5df7-b474-ed822b7bd89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388731} {:answer/id #uuid "acdf00ba-91a7-5488-8545-026a8d082afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388732} {:answer/id #uuid "4a195a52-8ce9-5053-b42d-97c4f5710244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388733} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1123b-b5b1-5929-a337-c49a7db7b5b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388734} {:answer/id #uuid "785e1b0c-75c6-5f36-9833-976480b92739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acdf00ba-91a7-5488-8545-026a8d082afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388732} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5915291-9a74-5ab8-ac85-36586c0071b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c94191a-43a1-5922-ab22-a6d441b65abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140474" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+7{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 11\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60933bd3-ca59-53dd-b9b0-1db1d440db09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388738} {:answer/id #uuid "e40a8c23-f4bf-5ce3-83ac-967fca612333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388739} {:answer/id #uuid "e15417fe-4e3a-5dc9-85cb-29bd49278518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 10 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388740} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f1420d-53ef-5213-998f-f80664faf30f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388741} {:answer/id #uuid "d01304c8-d666-5269-97b9-3cec349e53cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1f1420d-53ef-5213-998f-f80664faf30f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388741} :answer-field/id #uuid "862e16c6-55ff-5a1b-beec-90fa0f9883e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4d35477-b857-55bc-8644-6d12a78d9e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140481" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e78467bc-6d58-55b6-b616-7a98cfc1595f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388745} {:answer/id #uuid "8a411e8f-dcf2-5023-b8b1-2893ebae9b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388746} {:answer/id #uuid "bda3aac2-60f4-57a2-b56e-840056fdad2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388747} {:answer/id #uuid "774d2389-869e-5dbe-8c82-bf06c46f1dc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388748} {:answer/id #uuid "59efe942-c01a-58a8-8f3e-e98dfd8c64a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "774d2389-869e-5dbe-8c82-bf06c46f1dc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388748} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8a140a8-7c03-54de-818e-d027b7afb314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5605d4ea-f418-59f0-ade2-398cf6d0d3af" :question/math-academy-id "q-140496" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fd6b37-e011-5b68-8eaf-550d3f79239f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388752} {:answer/id #uuid "3780b78c-835d-5d26-b2d4-d00cc1c2e9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388753} {:answer/id #uuid "6747e699-9fae-5440-9b8f-204afa7a4f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388754} {:answer/id #uuid "64baed4a-7f09-5cd1-ac85-b8fc13d2dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388755} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc0d3b9-bd26-517d-a87c-907819d06b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64baed4a-7f09-5cd1-ac85-b8fc13d2dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388755} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b0905b-9058-5e15-92b6-332aab984e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a9407fa-0156-51d0-868b-a6bb68a8909c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140498" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "733eab11-2a26-5402-8df5-bba50c9beaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0430e0c4-a0b4-5049-ad23-35a8288d7cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-28,19,-19⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388758} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a43164-df74-5f59-9f11-3639b1e68c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-28,-19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388759} {:answer/id #uuid "fca0458d-7adb-55af-9b20-df320360f312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨28,-19,-19⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388760} {:answer/id #uuid "afb78719-5f2d-59cd-b63d-0e3512a7ce7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-18,19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388761} {:answer/id #uuid "cb139f18-1fd5-55e0-b900-e3e088cf39d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨18,19,61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb78719-5f2d-59cd-b63d-0e3512a7ce7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-18,19,-61⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388761} :answer-field/id #uuid "26765ce1-5d1e-5d12-ad0e-0e45e31383cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7427d773-9726-5cf6-8261-06dbd4949f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-1405" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨-3,10,4⟩$ and $v=⟨4,7,1⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(10\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 7)i-((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot 7-10\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =(10-28)i-(-3-16)j+(-21-40)k \\\\  & =-18i+19j-61k \\\\  & =⟨-18,19,-61⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66278037-da29-5141-928b-17cedf1b9cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/79858f004c47944a53fa4cb5f85efcf8ea562c2f1cdf519d06495bfa93c8c005.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388765} {:answer/id #uuid "73b2843f-e787-5fd9-b225-9e330c587a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/226990bb31d35aa5374377b3df6eb5cc41a93ee28c363f0be81e275b8ffc8d89.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388766} {:answer/id #uuid "7798430c-db7c-54b4-aac4-2cd892d04cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85c2faf0ffd691c2ac3b9934a21c965e7298dd06b1644a7f5d1f6e417696ee93.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388767} {:answer/id #uuid "1d917d91-606d-5c20-975e-911252dada6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/244afb477fee384908651d29418d155bc302bc9e3e6cb1a7cfe6718d9848f473.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388768} {:answer/id #uuid "c059bcde-8723-51d0-a72e-035bb81bc1e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/686c221d813d00c6bda5d9f1762fd8b327f85c8bc86a6e05aed5239028a896ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c059bcde-8723-51d0-a72e-035bb81bc1e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/686c221d813d00c6bda5d9f1762fd8b327f85c8bc86a6e05aed5239028a896ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388769} :answer-field/id #uuid "2960fe39-4842-53e6-a9f3-c1df3c8a0aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e199346-3281-5a3a-a0ea-8a0b8ede908a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140507" :question/problem "![](images/23/23d106faa9e3a72b27acc170cfb8ef2545465be7a80e88f833e6d20c654d8f64.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is an ellipse. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif the axes pass through the opposite vertices of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/68/686c221d813d00c6bda5d9f1762fd8b327f85c8bc86a6e05aed5239028a896ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61031960-3fc5-5fe0-95f0-0c7a9b0ff0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388772} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1349b9-6291-569c-83ec-402833cb45ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388773} {:answer/id #uuid "3a27a3d1-fcad-5965-8194-a093aa94c3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388774} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6b0ffa-adf7-5365-8a98-eaad1b7e3753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388775} {:answer/id #uuid "28d80879-289e-5661-89df-3713590efed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61031960-3fc5-5fe0-95f0-0c7a9b0ff0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388772} :answer-field/id #uuid "526936f7-c53e-5694-96ec-8ef1e6fae548" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "258bdd1d-0804-5b67-a22c-b1970d1d2fe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140570" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b7b81c97a842b82c2e4cbbd041883c1bd7f4f4ab58dc6592c12ec1cc0262620.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x(x+1)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-1,x=0$ or $x=3.$ Since $0\\in [-1,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral That Gives the Area Bounded by a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed59c0fa-145a-58c6-9183-7a618b9935a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388779} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2b15db-3e26-5206-ac2f-5ed2fb6e032f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388780} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6c7690-6ae3-52da-bc3d-2f05754b1818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388781} {:answer/id #uuid "66ed0fb5-f500-5d8a-908f-f603def527e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388782} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7a63c1-cb03-5469-bed3-2e5a4974c10b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b2b15db-3e26-5206-ac2f-5ed2fb6e032f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388780} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b97b39-9214-5cd5-be06-dad9c9ec0e0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb68cdd3-2757-5be6-bde1-043b15f42f06" :question/math-academy-id "q-140583" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b729d243b344df6b26757773bf0521dea80da34fc727541d24babd3732563cf.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{a}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{{x}^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [-1,\\frac{5}{2}],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6055e84-6449-5143-a2b3-46eff0358050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388786} {:answer/id #uuid "1f939b74-aaf7-502f-8564-0b661fd0e6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388787} {:answer/id #uuid "02624a8f-1b6a-5107-a65b-691283905860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388788} {:answer/id #uuid "a8527eb6-3fa6-5558-a401-122a4ccf2932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388789} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ff1e-f85f-5965-ad45-394bcfc443a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02624a8f-1b6a-5107-a65b-691283905860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388788} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7af0c55-c0c0-57b2-8764-cee03bfd3605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "869bbc7e-c91d-54b2-899a-a9769b330e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-140643" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e10680-715f-54cb-b957-9869e3d47c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388793} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2a59e1-9bb2-562b-a049-611da26b73f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388794} {:answer/id #uuid "6e74a9c5-64dd-5192-b624-6acb21743218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388795} {:answer/id #uuid "da3c1f63-621e-5ef9-a791-4bb9ed38d3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388796} {:answer/id #uuid "dde150c3-d6d6-546b-b5b9-453ed63b9c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe2a59e1-9bb2-562b-a049-611da26b73f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388794} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8fd4c90-98c8-5495-9558-4a6d0a8f3d69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9299a3a5-08db-5667-adf8-04da51bcb5fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-140836" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35450620-e1c1-5553-8d89-babffec49fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388800} {:answer/id #uuid "9509bf92-21de-58cf-a928-febc07388708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388801} {:answer/id #uuid "eba19a2d-d967-5a67-9c11-60ad3d49f2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388802} {:answer/id #uuid "ab55f8c6-2adf-5f61-aac6-7bf5271da29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388803} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388804} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf69525-3508-5fe7-99c0-fbb77383cf15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fb99b60-4344-5bc1-af82-6f45b3524b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-140911" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(\\frac{4}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a0e9a5-b999-5a3a-b3b4-0dbb1edcf165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388807} {:answer/id #uuid "44439493-9fe3-5198-a6e3-8d20d59399d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388808} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed466c4-440d-5823-8ca9-8d27260a4bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388809} {:answer/id #uuid "b9497cd6-2380-5312-9668-bbf279e3849a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388810} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6aec6a-c578-55ad-9ef6-c4d78ecfa9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44439493-9fe3-5198-a6e3-8d20d59399d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388808} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b5b70a-3bed-57f5-b549-2c7675473fbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b53edfc-e181-54eb-b6ba-ae6652fb15e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140951" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=7 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{84}[\\begin{aligned}21 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99270a04-7542-5545-92a3-cf13464ef8bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of an SVD With a Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e044b3e-0de0-5ba3-a1a8-c9c2af697d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388814} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1f2140-da8a-5a9a-ac9f-b7bd2a0eb0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388815} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae4669c-17b3-5dbd-b72e-85de2f92da19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388816} {:answer/id #uuid "b74a318b-f896-579d-a51c-c6a72c3ae206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388817} {:answer/id #uuid "e41f43ff-e62a-520e-80f3-03f0e2518535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1f2140-da8a-5a9a-ac9f-b7bd2a0eb0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388815} :answer-field/id #uuid "081c1bc8-0f12-5c26-a32f-4ede9647ceec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ded87b8e-27db-5dc9-a993-ea7145915541" :question/math-academy-id "q-140955" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44c68619-9cf3-5dd8-b3db-00c9730d3308" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Third Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166eb57b-154c-5dff-9b80-1f8ca0845010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388821} {:answer/id #uuid "3387a72d-c34d-5440-b9c6-c13590f43357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388822} {:answer/id #uuid "f74892c7-e706-5f81-ba76-aab1487434fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388823} {:answer/id #uuid "3938ff66-5101-512b-b7f9-9ec1cbda303b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388824} {:answer/id #uuid "b2140a7a-513d-531e-b6b9-0b2b2b2e4392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2140a7a-513d-531e-b6b9-0b2b2b2e4392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388825} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5c3228-7a26-54e7-a187-7cc419cf43c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e53efba-80e2-5d49-ba97-ee657aeabdf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140960" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=24,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-24I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-24[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-24I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea59a55-ca8c-5011-94bb-adc5e2783f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388828} {:answer/id #uuid "8e324f12-a685-504b-be59-f46dd3f2728f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "340" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388829} {:answer/id #uuid "02091894-fa17-5170-9a72-9c82ed0e6bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388830} {:answer/id #uuid "d0746dbd-50e5-51db-b5aa-9a4c741c97d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388831} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c66c2-72df-5f5b-aab3-269b3148afdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69c66c2-72df-5f5b-aab3-269b3148afdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388832} :answer-field/id #uuid "c17d838d-64b9-5408-9e52-5f894639d0cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a031dbeb-978c-5542-b4e9-e4e60f2dc984" :question/math-academy-id "q-141024" :question/problem "A study conducted on $3\\,400$ teenagers by the transportation department of a particular city reveals that teenagers' preference for the type of home-to-school transportation changes with time. The following table shows the results of this study.  \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Bus** | **Bicycle** | **Electric Scooter ** |\n| To: | **Bus** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Bicycle** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Electric Scooter** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of teenagers who used to go to school by bus now go by bicycle.  \n\nHow many teenagers would eventually go to school on electric scooters if the listed probabilities remained constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e35ad90-2f1b-5a63-97f8-d0ee68eca5d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269398} {:answer/id #uuid "2fde21b2-4071-56ca-975f-426d92a1cb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269704} {:answer/id #uuid "752357c7-c757-545e-b673-5e6aef138895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270310} {:answer/id #uuid "9758a9f8-4285-59af-9944-c703f3e02a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270604} {:answer/id #uuid "c828b14e-ea43-5244-865e-f22017cdf7a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fde21b2-4071-56ca-975f-426d92a1cb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269704} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc92cb1-fc14-5bd0-a868-45afb00eeb79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34dfcc70-f55e-5b7a-a24a-4dbf1116a030" :question/math-academy-id "q-14144" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{8}-18{x}^{2}-33x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 3.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{18}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm 3, \\\\ \\pm 18, & \\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119ad3fa-23a7-5e5d-bc13-95f12c9cc7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{dim(Ker}(T))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388835} {:answer/id #uuid "e884d3ae-2370-5149-9d5f-3fa50ab61a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|T(0)|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388836} {:answer/id #uuid "41a74594-cedd-52d7-9ef0-de9358885fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{nullity}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388837} {:answer/id #uuid "c811d82e-bdf5-5436-8612-447b29eaa0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{rank}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388838} {:answer/id #uuid "d80e9cbc-4ac1-5050-a14d-976c40a8c521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{det}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c811d82e-bdf5-5436-8612-447b29eaa0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{rank}(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388838} :answer-field/id #uuid "0722fc58-d6db-5b2f-baae-f5033e86a3ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "daefbd55-f267-5370-90c7-3c27c596267b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141455" :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & =n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{rank}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f0ec6b-9cb4-5246-9ac6-23b35e3b224e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of More Than Two Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178a154f-28d5-5531-aa55-5de126a1268f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QS}=a-b+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388842} {:answer/id #uuid "239674cf-b286-5120-a00c-9b51da020c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QS}=a-d+b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388843} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ece702-5b0b-5b43-b7f5-047d17972e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QS}=a+b+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388844} {:answer/id #uuid "9e16ff59-30c4-5f69-9f79-2b53deb0cb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QS}=c-d+b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388845} {:answer/id #uuid "a20c7190-fe5c-5615-9172-0fd61e174929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QS}=b+d+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239674cf-b286-5120-a00c-9b51da020c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QS}=a-d+b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388843} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9ada8c-55f8-56a9-ac33-7dce27c30eaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aba9a00f-cb4d-5b06-bebd-53c73877d87c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141480" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c681289ee631de241f876091878b84cd9497c5d8e71d27512f9a832c242329b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RS}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RP}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QS}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QS}=\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}+\\overset{\\to }{RS}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/31/317d78cec35bc3721ebbd64ac1ed53aa5b36c89101a170b93805d17c670f2673.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RT}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ff4b4292bb8a15551cb22da1f621684e9fdf24d35ae873b408dc612e5b3255e8.png)\n\n   \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QS} & =\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}+\\overset{\\to }{RS} \\\\  & =a-d+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c9dab48-f4ec-50d5-997f-66003dcb0d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388849} {:answer/id #uuid "78463560-21e0-5b25-866c-e90cd7340bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388850} {:answer/id #uuid "b9db4f34-08d3-52b7-b130-1953818d1cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388851} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388852} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcc5cac-e58c-525f-92e0-44013ae6b5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388852} :answer-field/id #uuid "32deee12-64c9-53b5-a191-c9595d854af8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd3a3fd-0ec4-5982-bdba-cd236ee44004" :question/math-academy-id "q-141811" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}+24x=-11.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}+6x+9$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+24x & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+6x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $36$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36+36 & =-11+36 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9ceefc-3aa8-5c13-90a1-e4a084f720cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e4d2de-99a8-5c5a-bcd7-bfbf63902855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1497c291a9a068a414503d3081f52c5df56019d496a3f6c16cc905e6a20b97fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388856} {:answer/id #uuid "a1177d92-f4ff-58b1-ae15-c2d12bba3b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e6af7aceed6e31bccd5f568d50ac1c897b98cc568d383ea559a8b61abc203e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388857} {:answer/id #uuid "a935f1aa-2e46-5432-9942-28834f14200a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1cc25938438d7ef2ad77bd5047a1417e5c0dba609e630bde7c3dee9dffa205ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388858} {:answer/id #uuid "184cb15f-141c-5d51-b648-0f352aa708e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388859} {:answer/id #uuid "98de12f6-db2b-5f19-b507-54a7283acb15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9febb7b2ffb18d3d4d1ea5976fb0de6ee164689d01d82cf89dfb83000febffb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "184cb15f-141c-5d51-b648-0f352aa708e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388859} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a641e6-d96d-56ee-a09e-0f810a98dfec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cee14fe-e375-5314-aab5-fe2b10bd1948" :question/math-academy-id "q-142097" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $4x-2y<6?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & <6 \\\\ -2y & <-4x+6 \\\\ 2y & >4x-6 \\\\ y & >2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=2x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=2x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny>2x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=2x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](images/2f/2fe69dace2487c1b6dc73fcd122fd358513d8ac9528b3195d2075f8b62c75b4a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a831251-1892-5348-acb0-08de00fc7410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec99abbe475fc356cfd36415012fe1a6e8e611895452b8e7b0f3f1b8457d61d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388863} {:answer/id #uuid "0b0f6713-300a-5873-adfe-ba515b646bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/801b9576f2c9840eac75479d8983eb8b12a8a1138da7bb361f417c14ffad6823.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388864} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3375a7-8226-5fcc-9260-c5266d4a00a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b68f8b6a6f5b72bbc9c11ca7c1af87965330cc72d5f9700486c80a4d22a08849.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388865} {:answer/id #uuid "bd596374-4a82-557d-9a74-1dcda7c6daf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bce1f17aa0fde89c6373bdc4499a1e2bb2c976f41b38d97568f7e73ed8d57dd8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388866} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcb4582-ca85-526e-b94c-668fb6055ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a8b3896667e3401eb610625ee71607c221ad7cdaf2d4ccea79bdbe54665ec7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a831251-1892-5348-acb0-08de00fc7410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec99abbe475fc356cfd36415012fe1a6e8e611895452b8e7b0f3f1b8457d61d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388863} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed07d46-b98a-5fa7-9c22-1b406322df54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60ebcac8-bc59-5dde-9394-be5d3c916d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-142112" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x+2y\\le 4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & \\le 4 \\\\ 2y & \\le -x+4 \\\\ y & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**. \n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line. \n\n![](images/8b/8b906cf167528f484109528a93732dc2b8d73558bb02f454bb520af87efde3c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f5b753a-7fbd-50d8-93d7-41cd57d255eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388870} {:answer/id #uuid "1126107e-186e-5040-9398-edb4d6acbbee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388871} {:answer/id #uuid "387d733c-7cfe-5c37-a088-20da0dfb5afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388872} {:answer/id #uuid "65011359-a368-5adf-95bc-7e28380bf0fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388873} {:answer/id #uuid "e576812b-f2a8-5232-87cf-c83d5c6c9f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f5b753a-7fbd-50d8-93d7-41cd57d255eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388870} :answer-field/id #uuid "c07f5031-bde3-5457-acc7-6e1f7faa31d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a3cbd65-d830-59dc-8511-5f72908033d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142485" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4\\times 5$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $B$ is formed by $2$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 2+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-2 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f0ec6b-9cb4-5246-9ac6-23b35e3b224e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of More Than Two Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7771de7-8c6e-52ad-b4c9-68d5d7384b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{TP}=a-c+d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388877} {:answer/id #uuid "8429482d-0790-5a76-b481-db7912ddc542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{TP}=a+b-d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388878} {:answer/id #uuid "bd08a8d5-44d2-527e-874c-a2443fc03474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{TP}=a-b+d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388879} {:answer/id #uuid "6faa05a4-315e-564c-a768-d3f28f8c5802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{TP}=a+b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388880} {:answer/id #uuid "05fd068b-455a-5591-bfe0-11931e031fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{TP}=b-c-d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8429482d-0790-5a76-b481-db7912ddc542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{TP}=a+b-d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388878} :answer-field/id #uuid "8128aa61-4d16-5191-a59a-1ec02e515f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0819890e-e160-59bd-9c05-1d68eddaa3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143000" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a89c73fd1e50e3c2d28694a366c79c75ad5f1bbf63a5c1333a7d7b62f5a4a0e9.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{TP}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TP}=\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP}\n$$\n\n \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0e6351dfbef6dadf6a6fb179f1545622ddaeab24c2c06dee03c87821304695c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TR}=-d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram. \n\n![](images/02/02899ac9c536b0020514780db617e29977a5dc4daf60f42d80ed671883327fe3.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TP} & =\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP} \\\\  & =-d+b+a \\\\  & =a+b-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5d1ab9e-6505-5ada-bb33-19f9bd4b6196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f18b75a1d71e01bd898e14a66bbec923abd988c72a8e9d69451c8edf1d16fc72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388884} {:answer/id #uuid "31235479-4830-5e58-9e6a-edbce55b30c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/418e4724130ac11b03ea2b293f36e81d712a5364ed5addf70ae2900ef196d650.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388885} {:answer/id #uuid "1531163f-68b6-5905-887d-2355ab556c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/7433238f7537aeb303f9a8bd595266a9525d286c1e162cd5b228eb0b861e382b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388886} {:answer/id #uuid "19ffdb9e-fbc8-562f-a75b-7b78615f6a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae74be6af41cbfd239e15f7bb2e2e09504dbb718c3f308ab41157a6251daad01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388887} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb9fd78-57ff-5323-aa10-946351508419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6bc89797cabfa5cc5e1baec5bf576fd81e4dd48fb9ff646009bd6cdad7e42c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31235479-4830-5e58-9e6a-edbce55b30c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/418e4724130ac11b03ea2b293f36e81d712a5364ed5addf70ae2900ef196d650.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388885} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ab8004-673e-5920-a0af-c5b35bc2a73c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea4847f7-e663-5fb5-abcf-705c57877607" :question/math-academy-id "q-143098" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $3x-2y\\ge 6?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & \\ge 6 \\\\ -2y & \\ge -3x+6 \\\\ y & \\le \\frac{3}{2}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{2}x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-3$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{3}{2}x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-3,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](images/ea/ea12ed8d62304ecdc90859d5cd206045cef852522ce16c67a9307ddbd755b3dc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cb6f60-ad52-57c8-9c89-d3f0205b2ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4fcb159117d6a3e3a2003e0d7e5611ba91c69399bded1830aca96a9a74fd092f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388891} {:answer/id #uuid "f79f3e9b-c241-51e1-b30f-a799e7db5d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f2/f2aca56b6bdc527fb130951c73c7307b3ff45dbb16b2f382907f5950e75e99be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388892} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2f9d5e-b0db-59d4-8789-82570f920c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/476463dc46da737a676d5c6c78e34f0870cc8b9874e991c9b2bf806d8618bc17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388893} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aabbfd-9a67-5be5-af23-944942874c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6f3c5c10ba14789418a5029dc75d9537050ec1c74ad75e73881131585763644b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388894} {:answer/id #uuid "6a35029d-76b2-5a4b-9128-3f5c86afcd1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/84edf48b555f43cfb4866b9798b03110a609c88e92e83ad1fca17a926718a67e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a35029d-76b2-5a4b-9128-3f5c86afcd1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/84edf48b555f43cfb4866b9798b03110a609c88e92e83ad1fca17a926718a67e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388895} :answer-field/id #uuid "a487c7ea-06dc-58aa-a36d-43a45051e3d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b79ca0d0-9411-53cc-9689-ca803ea493b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147522" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca7c2d5e2fe1729a3b076e63f9fe028ecb459b576f7843ab43e066a19ef89a81.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-9,3)$, $(-9,3),$ $(3,-1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/84/84edf48b555f43cfb4866b9798b03110a609c88e92e83ad1fca17a926718a67e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e83e8415-d72c-573e-b76a-b3960b3f8a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12850dda11d88566b9f62cb9a3d91b79c2b32900c76280257aae14aa69375ef9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388898} {:answer/id #uuid "f734ec14-b992-550d-bb55-937f5110cb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94247512fd446447144d808c76d00bc9c0d50d4175697597360117eca00b3cf1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388899} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebfa2a2-4d6c-5652-b4e4-dfda03193985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/aefad4c0112e05c1a014ab7a6c684207d047971eccff5fc1b1a637b7d09d8e94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388900} {:answer/id #uuid "cd49854b-8520-5e36-b6ab-2c38fd990d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc9a40db16e8434cadb89266518dcf56c6ed60213ecde42d237229466cdd99d0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388901} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbd1abd-93d2-53bf-81ba-f857f3652fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57bedcb6a7191770472af16526b2172ad0c8da718de33b606bc168c514a367d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f734ec14-b992-550d-bb55-937f5110cb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94247512fd446447144d808c76d00bc9c0d50d4175697597360117eca00b3cf1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388899} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf966af-1aa8-59dd-b018-c7432c0b7a60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de2d7c97-764c-58c0-b8ba-f8d09c210ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-147561" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d88ae8667df81624f0a28295c980f846ae88e9b4766fe0d0f692dd75509a56e9.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(1,4)$, $(5,20)$, and  $(2,8).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/94/94247512fd446447144d808c76d00bc9c0d50d4175697597360117eca00b3cf1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c77e01-4d1d-5c06-a480-d447276bb1d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/95fbb1a2298ebc809303d6c57a8440483cdd65748b090ea0b45d25ebad93bb80.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388905} {:answer/id #uuid "b10b56d0-0de6-5d02-bddd-cf895c4147d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f43f19bbe4213ba2f9d5bc51f52b747e1c7840a27607207f0ca56021c1914d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388906} {:answer/id #uuid "c66d7895-934c-5bb8-97d5-8056fed90107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388907} {:answer/id #uuid "beb8153f-9f67-5cdc-841a-d6318264c4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/378a24a8553ac7092b85f715394bf632b5e87aaf45c58ce3ff7ae66310b28f65.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388908} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5db3c4-68c8-55f4-9716-051a8b246d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0f0589a467b3d35947a6f501ff00d5296e05ae7e375800659986038db55c7577.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c66d7895-934c-5bb8-97d5-8056fed90107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388907} :answer-field/id #uuid "775d1a18-d6fd-53fc-a27b-7894cbc4846a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "babac56b-1bc2-59da-833f-f3070de287aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-147603" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/c6/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ea8228-51e6-5f85-b539-c1d15c0fe792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}-t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388912} {:answer/id #uuid "860a0600-6c2c-5c9e-b5c4-9b17db189976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388913} {:answer/id #uuid "23c91797-5d7f-5e12-aa9e-092abce95ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{t}^{3}+t-8)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388914} {:answer/id #uuid "463a4d0d-9541-5db1-8cdc-bef6635f6bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+2t-8)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388915} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1c4954-271d-5c20-a752-fbf6f8daf6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1c4954-271d-5c20-a752-fbf6f8daf6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388916} :answer-field/id #uuid "92df483e-25e7-5869-98dd-8c3813ab168b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7d9bec2-9225-5625-8a03-8734f22a69ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-148199" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2y)\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2-\\frac{x}{2}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2-\\frac{t}{2}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2-\\frac{t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{t}{2})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}-2(2-\\frac{t}{2})={t}^{3}+t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab06ac6-4b86-5107-beab-c84b66be2cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388919} {:answer/id #uuid "48d500af-45bd-5d76-b7e6-f7027293e1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388920} {:answer/id #uuid "eac27db1-c199-5099-9644-507bbdba7b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388921} {:answer/id #uuid "33e2affd-c422-5289-8198-ffb1637d1782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388922} {:answer/id #uuid "33ededb1-4f63-5237-92c5-029fc4f39566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48d500af-45bd-5d76-b7e6-f7027293e1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388920} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5a001a-2ddf-5fd9-a315-06e7e52536bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d45e7151-1e23-5fc3-b354-b72fb9448af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148205" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2{e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2{e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "282a1e18-465c-5b49-a7a6-bb88bbf45a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0547f4bf2530cbcc271e81ba501c3710b0359bb222d1e56014acac6675b515d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388926} {:answer/id #uuid "8456cd7c-7c9d-52da-9188-9ebd1f5b8263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd0a8472d9b1abff7090be8547c654f8842b6fc934f768d8c34fe208e4058990.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388927} {:answer/id #uuid "3afe2f32-8a9d-500f-881c-cf4d99dfc366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1cadc33591f95b5b66f50794e329768364708bb7f9de1e06d579ff1833be11d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388928} {:answer/id #uuid "5118601a-0d63-5cc8-975a-95840b50b90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c1521cfad08f4c1cc4a699066ec5dc84fd635c2f1d39f71dca5a8ba86533887.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388929} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb53477-aad7-59d6-916e-26b2f3531363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2f661772948f506a7fa8f0a205cf2e84b3a10dfc9bc35b695d572755ca35c917.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8456cd7c-7c9d-52da-9188-9ebd1f5b8263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd0a8472d9b1abff7090be8547c654f8842b6fc934f768d8c34fe208e4058990.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388927} :answer-field/id #uuid "e27ced13-67b5-592b-aa16-a6e20b3c3f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3557c58-b79b-5ef1-8132-f7d04ee3b456" :question/math-academy-id "q-149550" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(3-x){y}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=3$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/e4/e4d289aef282e076b062aab6d8f0f209396313e2c6403b2e27d1c0272ac90795.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/4c/4cef3532a8e70098c030f2341800f06e9e54e4074126a0a97aecdf1f1c5185d0.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ed172c5746b1b0afd2d9b7b3bbb21245115df164bfb7e34f4f530887376e28.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "851f1d07-48b6-5ddb-a46e-01bd86929f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e019652dcfef9bb06dcecb42c1544e93b2f2909cfc1a40aac1eee3cf24f73e7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388933} {:answer/id #uuid "110426ea-575e-5ad7-8973-a174878f9900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6cd862eaf47308e250abc6cd4016bcb84132de849b289753751840f9c44c107f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388934} {:answer/id #uuid "806a1e92-f8cc-5d2f-a944-e06375d7c37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/596e25c1d1f8a0e738d8853c17c77c2f6174a2c314a94ce1674f37b3a4c5c72b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388935} {:answer/id #uuid "73a0d172-7541-5fd9-90cf-501c16901c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/37000a15f2b6164932675c0f0e6dd78fa0ef3015dcc25b27d7dce139e8a1ce4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388936} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf1ad5a-a60f-5979-8ffe-63a2a960db79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/03b7fe2ba61cbe2608c37a78c82001f6a1fdebdb59f0be0785d55ee119ba8ff4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cf1ad5a-a60f-5979-8ffe-63a2a960db79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/03b7fe2ba61cbe2608c37a78c82001f6a1fdebdb59f0be0785d55ee119ba8ff4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388937} :answer-field/id #uuid "83dffd1d-727c-5d66-8134-97d6dbe4a971" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81bc3e7c-9070-5fbb-9de4-3490ca0d1dd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-149611" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/c4/c44c38febc908fe5e941978bf44bab2e2cb4bd250765950d84f9294c9c4c9c4b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/20/209576cd78e9beb1c0f7a9bce8267163df1668a830ffc5c06918f8a726600fbe.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | ${y}^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n  \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/43/433e949488dc7d20c4fda4d508941da3a12363750b7bf58eada642b98f130eb2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3fadd5-40a4-54fd-9df7-8c9527e439eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388940} {:answer/id #uuid "d624fdfa-6c18-5c32-9f83-6d774d9afeed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388941} {:answer/id #uuid "b49d27a8-69ed-556a-b313-79132cf2bc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388942} {:answer/id #uuid "af39217a-9741-57f5-a8af-aad29611cfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388943} {:answer/id #uuid "b73f5a3b-756e-5218-8d30-c5f269f48922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af39217a-9741-57f5-a8af-aad29611cfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388943} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef20f3c6-4187-5468-a4ad-e478b5888820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c996f89f-de6d-5978-856b-5758e6e8d01b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149682" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9841d5a0-2d6c-5f87-a27a-f94eefabc095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{θ}\\sqrt{2θ^{2} + 4θ + 1}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089604} {:answer/id #uuid "29dd10e3-97bd-56a6-b470-31427419f7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{θ}\\sqrt{4θ^{2} + 2θ + 1}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089605} {:answer/id #uuid "33a3e6d0-52bd-58d1-a159-6de1f2c26ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{θ}\\sqrt{θ^{2} + θ + 1}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089606} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbdd8df-eace-5463-a068-bbed35d03037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{θ}\\sqrt{2θ^{2} + 2θ + 1}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089607} {:answer/id #uuid "64f79122-83f5-593c-b841-41de68986368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{θ}\\sqrt{2θ^{2} + 2θ + 2}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089608}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0639070a-f499-59b7-b57a-59e78c70795f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089603}] :question/id #uuid "dd646452-89ff-5f38-8817-208b877e41c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-15144" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r = θe^{θ}$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc1884d7-f6fa-51d5-ab67-2d4dfe7971dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}+{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388947} {:answer/id #uuid "9df9f06f-758a-557e-a6d2-9c8187553a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{8}+2{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388948} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c21937-ce6c-561f-9c7f-beaefe07f762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{12}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388949} {:answer/id #uuid "29b74dcf-6de8-5e2b-a0c8-a8be7961cd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4{x}^{6}+2{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388950} {:answer/id #uuid "ba847247-1fbc-5b03-a578-a9ed456b0bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba847247-1fbc-5b03-a578-a9ed456b0bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388951} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fd233a-e678-5701-92e3-fd27713cdc1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891e62c5-2f59-5791-9c12-b3816ce7c5d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15403" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2}$ on $[0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{3}{2})6{x}^{2}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{1/2}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}(2{x}^{3}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a03fc3c-bdca-566c-900a-4b1ab771a160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388954} {:answer/id #uuid "f14fff00-638d-53fd-89ba-3776be4230ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388955} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d105e0-e02a-5f9a-8d42-8d11ca8aebea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388956} {:answer/id #uuid "094cb985-c7e5-5e73-8938-2f194e7a2eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+4{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388957} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd0f90f-470f-5cd1-9aa3-0c9a8f10f223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f14fff00-638d-53fd-89ba-3776be4230ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388955} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbb7be3-967d-5b2e-b4f3-8d11ad802511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "898e2d00-d1c0-5287-b1c1-c8753234b0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15405" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f74f28bf0b5bf2208b3cebf837c667cf9a9ae737d7eb758708a98548ffca8e3a.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=4{sin}^{2}⁡x$ on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14352d53-a485-5307-b112-104879c985d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388961} {:answer/id #uuid "05dada7d-111e-5f20-88ac-6afa8f9f9c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388962} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc051c8-5415-519f-a519-b6e946874fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388963} {:answer/id #uuid "614b492c-6971-59f9-aed8-3ee263cec78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388964} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b1478f-eae1-527f-ba01-427d7b9d60b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc051c8-5415-519f-a519-b6e946874fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388963} :answer-field/id #uuid "94da062c-31ea-5fc0-b57d-fe4122f66c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc07a546-4716-553a-9b4a-217a6ed81854" :question/math-academy-id "q-157051" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 24 \\\\ -24 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -24 \\\\ 24 & -7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-(2) & =-11 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466a14af-419f-527e-b9f3-45ccda2c256a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388968} {:answer/id #uuid "49467b16-c66d-5981-95f1-f41a27d0f189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388969} {:answer/id #uuid "33ffe7a1-67d2-5481-b344-193572a6847f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388970} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ab1c71-6000-5675-98fd-db1405a57c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388971} {:answer/id #uuid "162444cd-a8a6-5f12-b11d-5f7dc0e0ed43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ffe7a1-67d2-5481-b344-193572a6847f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388970} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b778d9-2ec0-5436-ace6-68397064b26b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd91585f-60ab-5f83-868d-d63a69da0526" :question/math-academy-id "q-158122" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1b1ee61-1308-5d61-b7ed-da45b7cfca5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388975} {:answer/id #uuid "f88bb249-b083-5351-9669-33db5c6b2547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388976} {:answer/id #uuid "d77cc695-36e6-5192-b27f-3e733685eb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388977} {:answer/id #uuid "55a3f1da-3779-59ce-ae0d-0dbecdb726b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388978} {:answer/id #uuid "053ae894-bb63-5ac6-b828-d9ea6bb6af7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55a3f1da-3779-59ce-ae0d-0dbecdb726b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388978} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6cde22-2f73-519a-945c-bffdd259e21b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd7403df-a496-59c6-8226-c07a3e6dd8d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158123" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b1ea4a-8431-5e9b-892f-bca7c95f80b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388982} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9edc5-9c1a-5e2a-b1fe-0d7188e07e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388983} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d236a8-2a2f-599a-b81e-073c9d88e925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388984} {:answer/id #uuid "e60312e7-b230-5a43-aa7d-233414826603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388985} {:answer/id #uuid "82820516-f5de-5854-9a60-bb231ec12fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9edc5-9c1a-5e2a-b1fe-0d7188e07e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388983} :answer-field/id #uuid "c310404e-e4a9-55ab-a9ab-4c596d9b583c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f3f0d39-46aa-5c30-a041-815de875f218" :question/math-academy-id "q-158129" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5fbd44c-edf5-56b4-9863-36a35f3b9ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9f4dd7da9adf7ec5580f4e866fdf094e90e87425ce435feae6dc41488e6518bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388989} {:answer/id #uuid "dce09265-cac3-50b1-9938-d4e109c7378c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e55fa4f75253935fb3f372dd82612b241d1133aceaa51666fdb0513decba8ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388990} {:answer/id #uuid "6e38f214-36b2-5226-88a7-d7314eed1c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/6431427a27685248942ae4ac998feeb8f38d210b63e7f4c429ce3a5bf9afd1e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388991} {:answer/id #uuid "aea45356-e834-5306-9e4b-0c712e35b96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04b8028809192321180e05f2e86c5e149b4870ddb0adea7e3191e2db33f12133.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388992} {:answer/id #uuid "283aafa9-a98b-5196-ab68-7113c77960e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91094f3f8fc9b21724e4e6d0f33e2ec0dc18782b3cc0aa1d4b1e4226e4bb93bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dce09265-cac3-50b1-9938-d4e109c7378c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e55fa4f75253935fb3f372dd82612b241d1133aceaa51666fdb0513decba8ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388990} :answer-field/id #uuid "c35e8875-bbf9-5e5f-bc9a-34467a284be5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddc33dd3-a1a1-567c-a9d1-d5d15d54e87f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158200" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2-x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-x\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ne 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne 2}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $x=2$ and shade *right* and *left* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/23/2371c9724d66e0f7d74c45e173f9625a986615185c10d55c029976baa82d0877.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c91f7252-2ff5-527f-9655-70e7dabdfa63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/89076b709a935f384acab2609f2d046123ceb3984c5ae60818f908525ad5e53d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388996} {:answer/id #uuid "34f1e16e-cbc1-5a7f-b808-dd9fc70fa781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/4030364abd4fd5a8f21e4ac358adb077ed56fb2a33f14ed49d4131e7ab36af6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388997} {:answer/id #uuid "8295865c-6ec5-5877-93c7-08b4a9de7247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43693fc51d09f6408198a74dc5672a2904b1ad0d45f6abc7f1f4aad0b2764a5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388998} {:answer/id #uuid "5accd97b-35b2-571d-8505-8db9329051a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f8/f843c386c730f85e7c3511e4fd8631b01f77b998f4e99fe19ed9783e4c4b6cbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388999} {:answer/id #uuid "5eeca2ff-1397-558c-b26b-156e2af27244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/44e6511542b39bb3946095ff28c7a82ab3e35b5e50d648aba246413e6fea00c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c91f7252-2ff5-527f-9655-70e7dabdfa63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/89076b709a935f384acab2609f2d046123ceb3984c5ae60818f908525ad5e53d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388996} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8830a22-e4bc-585f-b4d8-653840c79639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa9a90c8-68ec-5392-a3c8-0ae9ab63850c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158202" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-2x>0\\,⟹\\,y>2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2x}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $>$ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $y=2x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/42/42e2faf3df63b038b57d2fddc9b7ef2c2e720dae6e0a8524d602b7ac93681b3d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "613e7132-2128-56b5-bc00-f53656e511b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/65/65e29ff9fda5ad25fbbd68121d0c1a86e27a41de8890af31425208d7c38f2c2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389003} {:answer/id #uuid "344a137c-9b7d-593b-8372-97e276d83d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c3/c30be01de07727a44fce390cfc2c56ddeed8071a7a7d64abec831a31aa2079fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389004} {:answer/id #uuid "73c75e4a-3588-5716-a610-2b5c06d6af3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/4789f1e3a4fbe0c6f23980010a0222905cb46b4ef105b02e7fba0b59fc570eeb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389005} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8633ad-7e8b-588d-bda6-758ae14482e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389006} {:answer/id #uuid "04f3aa5b-1769-54da-88b9-15a178728381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fefbca84defd52b9fecd3b13b37cb95a744b4ddedd5a570ad554b6a251cd6077.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f3aa5b-1769-54da-88b9-15a178728381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fefbca84defd52b9fecd3b13b37cb95a744b4ddedd5a570ad554b6a251cd6077.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389007} :answer-field/id #uuid "60958810-5683-5293-9f68-2735d4937038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9419d3b0-0595-5357-8a67-2ff487c67499" :question/math-academy-id "q-158205" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,(x.y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/70/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89065094-1124-5c4c-b1ba-c19584e623f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0 < y < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190967} {:answer/id #uuid "c816bd8f-37c2-5162-aeab-cc70b2a140d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190968} {:answer/id #uuid "0a26dc4f-08a6-5c47-b9e4-8d674fe40cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1 < y < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190969} {:answer/id #uuid "364d78c9-1933-54db-af3c-1aebdf1418b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190970} {:answer/id #uuid "765c7aa5-d5d4-57d2-9fa7-98220be62120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1 \\le y \\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190971}] :answer-field/id #uuid "500766af-f20f-5af3-8f07-21325ccfaf03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190966}] :question/id #uuid "846d6059-ad4f-5e01-8a65-610c7d23e3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158314" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is\n$intA = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 < x < 1, A A A A A}$.\nWhat is the missing expression?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59df8c83-20c2-59c0-bf86-ff91d435fad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389010} {:answer/id #uuid "5859b576-61ad-5ce5-a21e-ba9d528365f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389011} {:answer/id #uuid "28fb4c7a-5133-53e0-ba21-c179f97f1979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389012} {:answer/id #uuid "e56685f2-15d8-5cc1-8330-230a60f3bdc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389013} {:answer/id #uuid "24c325b9-2c1e-539a-94de-23336697b0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c325b9-2c1e-539a-94de-23336697b0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389014} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ddbeff-2df7-55fa-abb1-f8f4f1b0d152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51493b5e-5437-5dda-a56f-ba4f747b7597" :question/math-academy-id "q-158362" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77509710-558f-57d7-aa0b-d2c981268322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389017} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea03b7e-02ea-578c-913e-693044d40660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389018} {:answer/id #uuid "16afd48a-f37f-5caa-9c97-076511b40cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389019} {:answer/id #uuid "a84f2641-ea85-514d-b1d7-5c6aa198ca55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389020} {:answer/id #uuid "315b04de-d0f3-59f7-b632-32a7cf9a8ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16afd48a-f37f-5caa-9c97-076511b40cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389019} :answer-field/id #uuid "68cf1131-7e03-5583-b36c-8f53f5e6d06d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed199a9d-a3a2-5954-968b-924b5a1b21d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-158372" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14\\cdot 9-9\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6d1de36-c45f-576e-a7f1-ddd4556e6e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\cos θ, y = 49\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149130} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e6c06b-fbd2-58df-8e45-287b8f1bd672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\sin θ, y = 7\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149131} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f202dd-0ca4-50e2-93ae-12d42b873745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 7\\cos θ, y = 2\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149132} {:answer/id #uuid "bebafa99-24a7-5707-8536-24d126c9d58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 7\\sin θ, y = 2\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149133} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9a2b8e-346c-559b-aa35-5c942ebd9ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\cos θ, y = 7\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149134}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d808a2-555f-5182-9d76-592cf8cf6ea8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149129}] :question/id #uuid "46c88046-0f62-50bb-8a31-ae13a6c535ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-15856" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7, 0)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e33f13-bf32-57fc-9e33-1c3365d2b549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389024} {:answer/id #uuid "45edddc3-4ab5-5f20-b063-71b14cfab775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389025} {:answer/id #uuid "a609d06b-6fba-58d1-ac0c-77b3c30ee67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389026} {:answer/id #uuid "27df9d1e-2610-5e00-b40d-bca3d5157238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389027} {:answer/id #uuid "428468dc-0f82-5095-b6bf-387fc8aa1e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "428468dc-0f82-5095-b6bf-387fc8aa1e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389028} :answer-field/id #uuid "991862b1-972b-5127-a2c8-a89e2bc72fa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ede69c8f-2758-5c85-a62b-6b678eca588f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159976" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-6-(-4)}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600a43db-7ad7-59a7-8a4e-811756e9e090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/4367227477398d54e05d978d1499aea7f0dabb42ab9223b21cbec43c5ada55c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389030} {:answer/id #uuid "af57ed6d-87af-5a9e-aba0-d344b0beded2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12ded9e8002035e126364e41d0dffc1abd6332b3496b4db9da4a68e116e7492e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389031} {:answer/id #uuid "f1817307-acca-5145-b9f8-f957482ffe59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389032} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e1dfd6-e586-58b6-a3b2-fb619c6f4782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25f5503b720084033be843eb1bce7adfe3bc59c39f5f634074d4e414079c9c36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389033} {:answer/id #uuid "15d16791-9d02-5cda-8ca2-b435a7d594dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33293e18f3b42154b3a21dd627e7f06fbcf443b1251ac1a9ce6e5cb6a47c5971.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1817307-acca-5145-b9f8-f957482ffe59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389032} :answer-field/id #uuid "318f82be-54f2-5f7d-8b28-47015fa276c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91008bb7-2af5-5c56-89d5-09e110f3df57" :question/math-academy-id "q-160353" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u(1+{0}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,u(1+{1}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,2u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de3fa2fa-304c-583a-8c73-a5ff1db972e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Rectangular Region Onto a Given One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9de898ca-f275-5cd4-9ec3-50dfbb539dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194130} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d44885-decf-580a-84ea-8fbdeff2cff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194131} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d3b428-2c31-5423-8491-e4f98ea25303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194132} {:answer/id #uuid "110532ce-a9e3-5bb5-8aa8-502f6f717961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194133} {:answer/id #uuid "24c330e5-9b44-577d-b85a-3ff91e5c25ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{u}{v}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194134}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec90679-7ec5-5181-8643-eeea485fc9f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194129}] :question/id #uuid "f0c60c12-3acb-5c5a-8aba-c02d0db96c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160559" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n$y = x, y = 3x, y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}$.\n\nA transformation $T$, where\n\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n$T: (u, v) → (\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}, A A)$,\nwhat could be the missing expression?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e468aabf-e6e4-5599-81b1-17dc6d355954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389036} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeedbec-6bdf-59e7-9b2b-b9d5e4e3ee50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389037} {:answer/id #uuid "a24ff250-c577-5a6e-8589-b7fa78f56034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389038} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf74745-74d7-5023-8d2f-494bd3a4b62a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/154f70201a5a00737d8df61952f5600ff91a2373df07b0b54680fe84e048402c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389039} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a52325-f624-5716-886a-b507033c0141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/090bab0ac1c6356010d83a6ad54c59b53b7dd12062fb5e331a936ce3f3a1b411.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a24ff250-c577-5a6e-8589-b7fa78f56034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389038} :answer-field/id #uuid "391991ce-c7bf-55cb-8662-29196fd230b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4471fd6f-9a7d-5e2e-b974-bcbd5ed1ceb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-160601" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & >0 \\\\ -4uv & >-4 \\\\ uv & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532e99cb-0323-59bb-ad1c-e8bff42b0ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf86cad03c80018ac3976a64780650c827d7c93a0889286c711c5aa69d2b899f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389043} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3e2cda-e1ba-5c1b-a2da-0575b6308eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f2/f2beba76039ead47c83c93aaad5f9c4717a4abd2b803e65157f23d770f4f9181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389044} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b2207e-4858-513b-ade9-581214549259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3edafbd6273f8058114c606c8b2a288e52b348d560867b6ea90e8b981b486904.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389045} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e3de21-40c5-54cd-8f01-712442275c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a78a3e98da4c602b4e8a2ebbe8860f75e507ec2ec5c20df0e59100f96a099ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389046} {:answer/id #uuid "28763a58-8bf2-57ac-a7a6-92284799cb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82091407834f5d7aa8caf6e01cb800a3f7b1999cb0c6e568c899d43479b1d7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5e3de21-40c5-54cd-8f01-712442275c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a78a3e98da4c602b4e8a2ebbe8860f75e507ec2ec5c20df0e59100f96a099ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389046} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cfb7bdd-9bd1-5e2f-ba15-29688fcfe868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efaa3158-b486-5ce8-9ecd-d7b2e13036d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160621" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b75a7519c7953b7a3c5bd5b5e61e961ca26c3f0dc9e1aab32bf2c639dd95325f.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f1/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8ea257c65769c4d6f67f55ce6533c1bfdfe45debcf1ba532b9ec3f717238507a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87084364-abe0-5ffb-9ed0-31fcedec776e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/05963ad5c8caadae06a2dd07faaf217f7927d0698eaff91a219837a4171e4de8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389050} {:answer/id #uuid "f8720d56-2e77-5486-97d7-955b4f4b682c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/63d73b449ca97cfad5b54cad11e53b77554da361dbe3e33c5b6f58b23b802fec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389051} {:answer/id #uuid "a1071b7a-73e9-5844-9e1c-3d9330374aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/805c480c5341b6fbf3e8d5720187fead535db2a651939572a3a9faf453726087.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389052} {:answer/id #uuid "41953237-f9fc-559c-82aa-b62b7ee3907a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/db/db5060bb186aee7860ad69d8a5c4516aa3e01e43c8f42b98f2cf260eb606357e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389053} {:answer/id #uuid "b1243d75-3778-5b5c-9522-8c36c581f9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1071b7a-73e9-5844-9e1c-3d9330374aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/805c480c5341b6fbf3e8d5720187fead535db2a651939572a3a9faf453726087.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389052} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a0e1aa-f1db-5de6-9ec9-bf93b2ab6a7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c94ecad8-4e36-5921-9c83-d90975b4539d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160626" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c32886c161e347daa9bf6fa7215f8a09abc4334632c33020c31ade6dfc4e7315.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f1/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/26/266af89426f86d22bc634a231c46a5ad89be14d06bc300a5fa20597998c826fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8867d91-bcda-5489-8280-49d891cc31ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ead70b80d9256e1211addc404613f2dfe4a19baf822cbd77f429572ffc9a3a6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389057} {:answer/id #uuid "48a91081-fad4-5761-a448-423550b39ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70dfc0c8412bfae3bdbb7128fc021199f11d63d6f4ee264bea20da70dd18316c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389058} {:answer/id #uuid "3faab04f-ba07-5e75-a43f-8198da358c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a1923eec2b66ef4eb861ec42b00ea48a3285aaaf3255c42d12c001c9cde32f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389059} {:answer/id #uuid "fe51163e-7d12-5501-9934-728d90d9412d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09b2ae9333118de0621a5f29610b6a83689fadaeb9c05e7a30b2799b12a1bd58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389060} {:answer/id #uuid "7c87cd81-f329-516d-8bf5-dc6b1dc8dca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/23/23f63717c8d356c698137359824112511b0bbd5ec04c59e47d22500d842d03b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48a91081-fad4-5761-a448-423550b39ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70dfc0c8412bfae3bdbb7128fc021199f11d63d6f4ee264bea20da70dd18316c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389058} :answer-field/id #uuid "328c6cdb-dc56-550a-b1a6-60351bc4df59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04e79ef7-7090-527f-95f0-0f13ae5b3650" :question/math-academy-id "q-160627" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bd98626bc49cc3c58e503d5a7373b6af0fcce721cfdf82ff372eaa57d157dda.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/77/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5ff1c1a7c99b08d04d2ac58d9f8195ee29c8b564873a9abfb2635e93cec1968.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca8e913-bddb-5b6e-84f9-856530467337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4ee5f1df8f74ca6412d4f02fba7258b2b672a8bc80ef13617d4b327f8859e007.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389064} {:answer/id #uuid "6e15c43c-6c08-5aab-a751-b59a2c11ace9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/941f34cbed3f3d6a9957b8520b36d6cbdc20c6241d2e407381ac86d3241a3349.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389065} {:answer/id #uuid "248188a9-928e-5c87-ad62-46c21aa9491c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/662c1937641b84653920b9c4e8cfac8168470d077641d001ebd258a0ffff34ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389066} {:answer/id #uuid "e01d5167-cb9b-5f33-8166-a5cf7601da02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6d/6d514d79a788e4512370310eab371dc334e37fc20aaffdf3ad93ebe632b84d4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389067} {:answer/id #uuid "83f448db-dc49-5111-9d66-3f3b3fa4d186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f448db-dc49-5111-9d66-3f3b3fa4d186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068} :answer-field/id #uuid "c10a4451-d9b8-5b5f-a00f-c4f4fbd71af3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b264343-8b82-57fd-9f36-893b59fb2172" :question/math-academy-id "q-160628" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,{1}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,1-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-0,\\,{0}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/7f/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaf80778-6e4d-52c2-a5db-4a2ef680da77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19650cf966c3c455fa859877b4718c08aea8a343876e5817c625618098a84730.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389071} {:answer/id #uuid "c91635d8-70ba-5406-b235-c716cd8cb5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9dc7559632cbecf450389f362ca478d40576ac9646489fda281519c48f49eb16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389072} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d4992f-3bd8-5933-9744-bd2784689f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/565227489ba6ae7a657a4b2f1e0133bc117e0e666557f58f59ec0272ad8c574e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389073} {:answer/id #uuid "d9925307-ecfb-5b78-8679-f58cb68b9e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389074} {:answer/id #uuid "f94a3624-0a44-5d1c-a294-b6849858edb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eb6908aea50ad1c314efefdaaf922a8c637673a38f76ee8aadcbdc361f44ff78.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9925307-ecfb-5b78-8679-f58cb68b9e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389074} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4309d7-1981-54e2-82f3-7e4f837d1ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e3c0bea-5d6e-5a25-bcb3-abdcd207f44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160632" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{0}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,1) &  \\\\  & =(1+{v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{1}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+1,\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,0) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c41f979-3dd4-5a07-b9d1-7abcad42f253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48a8dc30e5e9dcbd14673f1b120af39d706ca9cfe60dcdfa4a45b5bf2d33f0fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389078} {:answer/id #uuid "143cba19-e67e-53ad-9f8e-752d3c856632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c50d53ea64f295bed17ba1f9143374cec7dd7b9ff5128861c17ab3730d5d3f79.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389079} {:answer/id #uuid "0a208d98-ea42-5195-8140-855e635b8261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a2614c282a7cd82ff52ecacd7135f9c95bce5df5cfa9a06f2824b63c60c6eb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389080} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f9b1c9-0f68-5311-9f52-7592e7ceb3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/abdf69cc018c8a0bc7ce6e73f3cca70cb26400ccc5f618f94e05620fc2767bb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389081} {:answer/id #uuid "a18dd9cb-2ae4-5ea9-b8bd-4c6d1b80e29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/0204d60ae19340ddd417982aef69b1fa67c6f6c3ac78142735726aeea7fb2b24.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a208d98-ea42-5195-8140-855e635b8261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a2614c282a7cd82ff52ecacd7135f9c95bce5df5cfa9a06f2824b63c60c6eb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389080} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2a36fa-1df6-5380-bca3-547e89ee67e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17778faf-867d-5bf4-a58a-bee309a2767d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160634" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v(u+1),\\,y={v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0(u+1),\\,{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v(1+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(2v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1(u+1),\\,{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v(0+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a2614c282a7cd82ff52ecacd7135f9c95bce5df5cfa9a06f2824b63c60c6eb2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be70133-e143-555a-b316-c857cb78e325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169120} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c62bb4-f2b7-5a28-bab7-5df3a9504149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169121} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf2f94a-1a8b-5d99-8a70-f07f6c82f8fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169122} {:answer/id #uuid "0966e2fa-eeb2-5012-9af2-d57f5e7b0e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169123} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1bec9b-3572-5c9b-a9e1-b8bf0036f7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169124}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5413c5-cd70-5f06-8ea5-ec7c6d11e516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169119}] :question/id #uuid "25e44fde-444d-5880-ba2e-43dd4bc039db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160711" :question/problem "$∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS = ∬_(D)AAAAAAAdA$\nConsider the surface integral of the function\n$f(x, y, z) = xyz$\nover the parametric surface\n$r(u, v) = ⟨u, v, uv⟩$\nfor $(u, v) ∈ D⊆R^{2}$. What is the missing expression?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f632e1-bc4a-5e20-9d42-993ea0ac140f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 2yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163841} {:answer/id #uuid "d9597573-13cf-5b29-80fc-a0cec25c3d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163842} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf7721d-433b-562f-8b02-6dc0eaf18ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy - yz + 2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163843} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a59231-39be-5bd9-b000-7fd5bea7b50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy + yz + xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163844} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfc1e63-d79d-5c0a-a85e-bb0f5321e73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 3yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163845}] :answer-field/id #uuid "800fbb75-73ff-5c83-b570-3144244101d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163840}] :question/id #uuid "7492e5fe-6cc9-5d4b-8d1f-20d212147cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $n^(~)$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨z, y, x⟩, n^(~)(x, y, z) = ⟨2y,-z, 2x⟩, n = (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖)$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(S)\\frac{AAAAAAA}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f97954b-fab9-555e-a63d-0b7ff6619a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82557cc5a33963a7ec6d7910da8dc1409fd2cdc1245b00be009f7a237bee6c3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389085} {:answer/id #uuid "4f425ff2-e50f-5b19-ab10-931b2a0a25ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389086} {:answer/id #uuid "35760d42-f2ba-5032-8da1-1ca1d76889e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/128955fddc503b24eb73c5a321a803a4318f16e83e88f1d28be0a0e621f926c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389087} {:answer/id #uuid "fde379d9-5306-507a-a69f-f50ff3931c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b2026b5c41f64f72aa9d1c849f9357f4192860545e9a41241c2a0f87d33db6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389088} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a07a20-8567-5538-9f06-4d6de140aa40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/709f9c571b81383cb436570ea4c63575a2fb7e83b1c2e377a6347f54c8ceea35.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f425ff2-e50f-5b19-ab10-931b2a0a25ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389086} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e6b72a-de31-55a4-8613-ca44de670c50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31d74e75-fb66-5eb8-9774-1cd2896eeaa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160909" :question/problem "![](images/95/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f729c97f-3cdd-5808-9965-99a3a4b12cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/993c8929f019f9753f8b9e0b6662248bf9f41b8f743d423ef11c4838922de17c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389092} {:answer/id #uuid "338a9924-5265-5b6b-97fe-23afe4602a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/36cd7eaa8f2be8e3c8be883f7de52c2ea8bb87b2ac40b6ae0f9de22598d53ef6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389093} {:answer/id #uuid "2b492f1e-6fd0-5371-8d50-691cf2e7acb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389094} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee13b28-0f93-56ff-92a7-86287c2f6160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c093c8b5b55bcf5c50b0fc6c60d47256c9aaf86b8114ba29d6cef5def8cd310.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389095} {:answer/id #uuid "9e45936a-1c95-55d8-9812-13920f362a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/ce5b81c795019bb779f766d70a42872ef15aac38440a4c38876c76842b3a0c69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b492f1e-6fd0-5371-8d50-691cf2e7acb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389094} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd89d67-b436-5e5f-8398-6f045a7d552b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62187b1b-2a1e-5c4c-8542-39321690d816" :question/math-academy-id "q-160912" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f436ee381cafdb41a4a8df2bc68c06751d7d0a6d55641303caf04dcb4cab6619.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=2$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png)"}]}
